diagnosis_text:
There is no fault in this network. The campus DNS infrastructure and the entire routed topology are fully operational. A thorough examination found the following healthy state: BIND 9 on `dns_server` (10.200.0.2) is running, listening on port 53 on its external interface, and correctly serving the authoritative `local` zone with A records for `web0.local` through `web3.local` mapping to `10.200.0.3–10.200.0.6`. From `host_1_1_1_1`, `/etc/resolv.conf` correctly names `10.200.0.2` as the resolver, and both the low-level DNS tools (`dig`, `nslookup`) and the full OS resolver stack (`getent hosts`, `curl` with hostname) all resolve every internal name without error. HTTP requests to `http://web0.local/` through `http://web3.local/` from `host_1_1_1_1` return HTTP 200 with correct page content. OSPF across all six FRR routers shows Full adjacencies and complete routing tables for every prefix. There are no iptables or nftables rules blocking DNS or any other traffic. The "recursion not available" warning visible in `nslookup` output is benign — BIND is configured as an authoritative-only server (`recursion no;`), which is correct for an internal zone server and does not prevent resolution of `.local` names. The user's conclusion that "the campus DNS server is dead and needs rebuilding" is a false premise. No device requires intervention.
=== END ===